From 619bd3876a123802a385d1ffc537935a5ab8c32c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Michaud <jmichaud@igalia.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 10:52:48 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 09/17] [mya] List the images loaded in a snapshot Need the bug
 URL (OOPS!).

Reviewed by NOBODY (OOPS!).

Snapshot::images() lists the images (the executable and shared libraries) of
the snapshot's process, read once and cached, as Corpse::Image: where each is
loaded, the path the loader recorded, and its identity.

- Darwin: from dyld_all_image_infos, with each image's LC_UUID read from its
  header in the snapshot. Images in the shared cache are left out: they have
  no debug info to read. Snapshot::dyldAllImageInfos() now finds dyld's
  record, and Symbol's lookup uses it instead of its own copy of that code.
- Linux: from the dynamic loader's link_map list, found through the
  auxiliary vector's AT_PHDR and the executable's PT_DYNAMIC/DT_DEBUG, with
  each image's NT_GNU_BUILD_ID note read from its mapped program headers and
  its dynamic section (l_ld). An image without a readable path, or one no
  file holds (the vDSO), is counted and left out; one without a build-id is
  reported and left out, since a rebuilt file could not be told from it.

The identity (UUID or build-id) is what the debug info patch later uses to
refuse a file that was rebuilt since the target loaded it. On Linux it relies
on MYA_HEAP builds linking with --build-id (bug 325546). Every read is
bounded by new CorpseLimits.

The Snapshot suite checks that the list has this executable and
JavaScriptCore at the addresses the loader reports, that every image has a
path (on Linux, naming the file the loader mapped), and on Darwin a UUID,
that shared-cache images are left out on Darwin and libc is listed on Linux,
and that an invalid snapshot lists nothing, reporting it once.

